WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.621
Caching is a prefix match.

2
00:00:01.901 --> 00:00:07.640
When you mark part of a request with cache_control, the API stores the processed prefix up to that point.

3
00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:15.856
A later request whose prompt starts with exactly the same bytes up to that point reads the stored prefix instead of processing it again.

4
00:00:16.136 --> 00:00:17.992
Three rules govern everything else.

5
00:00:18.272 --> 00:00:21.365
Render order is tools, then system, then messages.

6
00:00:21.645 --> 00:00:25.059
The cache key covers everything before a breakpoint in that order.

7
00:00:25.339 --> 00:00:31.632
A breakpoint on the last system block therefore caches your tool definitions and system prompt together.

8
00:00:31.912 --> 00:00:34.813
Any byte change invalidates everything after it.

9
00:00:35.093 --> 00:00:42.112
A different timestamp, a reordered JSON key, one extra tool: the prefix no longer matches from that point on.

10
00:00:42.392 --> 00:00:45.571
The docs’ invalidation table adds settings to the list.

11
00:00:45.851 --> 00:01:04.475
Changing tool definitions invalidates the whole cache; changing tool_choice or adding an image invalidates the message part; changing thinking or effort settings always invalidates the message part; and requests at different speeds (fast mode versus standard) don’t share cached prefixes.

12
00:01:04.755 --> 00:01:09.960
Caches are also scoped per model, and isolated per workspace on the Claude API.

13
00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:12.523
Small prefixes don’t cache, silently.

14
00:01:12.803 --> 00:01:22.531
Each model has a minimum cacheable length (512 tokens on the current Opus, Sonnet, and Haiku models, per the caching docs at the time of writing).

15
00:01:22.811 --> 00:01:27.952
Below it, the request runs uncached with no error, and both cache meters read zero.

16
00:01:28.232 --> 00:01:30.856
Mechanically you have two ways to mark the cache.

17
00:01:31.136 --> 00:01:41.120
Automatic caching is a top-level cache_control field; the API places the breakpoint on the last cacheable block and moves it forward as a conversation grows.

18
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:46.072
Explicit breakpoints go on specific content blocks, up to four per request.

19
00:01:46.352 --> 00:01:58.853
Use explicit breakpoints when the end of the prompt is unique per request (a one-off question, retrieved rows): put the marker at the end of the shared part, or every request writes an entry nobody reads.
